  "ElementCount": 62,
  "SourceElements": [
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-001",
      "SourceSection": "16.1: Find the Perfect Squares",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The expression \\(x^2+8x+16\\) is equivalent to \\((x+4)^2\\). Which expressions are equivalent to \\((x+n)^2\\) for some number \\(n\\)?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-002",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Elena and Han solved the equation \\(x^2-6x+7=0\\) in different ways.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-003",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Elena said, “First I added 2 to each side:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x^2 - 6x + 7 + 2 = 2\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle (x - 3)^2 = 2\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "I can find the square roots of both sides:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x - 3 = \\pm \\sqrt{2}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Which is the same as:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = 3 \\pm \\sqrt{2}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "So the two solutions are \\(x=3+\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(x=3-\\sqrt{2}\\).”",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Han said, “I used the quadratic formula:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{\\text- b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4 \\boldcdot a \\boldcdot c}}{2 \\boldcdot a} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Since \\(x^2 - 6x + 7 = 0\\), that means \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = \\text- 6\\), and \\(c = 7\\). I know:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 4 \\boldcdot 1 \\boldcdot 7}}{2 \\boldcdot 1} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{8}}{2} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = 3 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{2}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "I think the solutions are \\(x = 3 + \\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{2}\\) and \\(x = 3 - \\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{2}\\).”",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Do you agree with either of them? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "16.2: Different Ways to Solve It",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Under what circumstances would solving an equation of the form \\(x^2+bx+c=0\\) lead to a solution that doesn’t involve fractions?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Solve each quadratic equation with the method of your choice. Be prepared to compare your approach with a partner‘s.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(x^2 - 10x + 25 = 0\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(x^2 + 14x + 40 = 0\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(x^2 + 14x + 39 = 0\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(3x^2 - 5x - 11 = 0\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider the quadratic equation:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x^2 - 5x = 25\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "It is often most efficient to solve equations like this by completing the square. To complete the square, note that the perfect square \\((x+n)^2\\) is equal to \\(x^2+(2n)x +n^2\\). Compare the coefficients of \\(x\\) in \\(x^2+(2n)x +n^2\\) to…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} x^2 - 5x &= 25 \\\\ x^2- 5x + \\frac{25}{4} &= 25 + \\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\left(x-\\frac52\\right)^2 &= \\frac{100}{4} +\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\left(x-\\frac52\\right)^2 &= \\frac{125}{4} \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The two numbers that square to make \\(\\frac{125}{4}\\) are \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{125}}{2}\\) and \\(\\text-\\frac{\\sqrt{125}}{2}\\), so:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x-\\frac52 = \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{125}}{2} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "which means the two solutions are:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = \\frac52 \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{125}}{2} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-033",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Other times, it is most efficient to use the quadratic formula. Look at the quadratic equation:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-034",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle 3x^2-2x = 0.8 \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-035",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We could divide each side by 3 and then complete the square like before, but the equation would get even messier and the chance of making a mistake might be higher. With messier equations like this, it is often most efficient to use the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-036",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = {\\text-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac} \\over 2a} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-037",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "To use this formula, we first need to put the equation in standard form and identify \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\). Rearranging, we get:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-038",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle 3x^2 - 2x -0.8 =0 \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-039",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "so \\(a=3\\), \\(b=\\text-2\\), and \\(c=\\text-0.8\\). We have to be careful to pay attention to the negative signs. Using the quadratic formula, we get:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-040",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = {\\text-(\\text-2) \\pm \\sqrt{(\\text-2)^2-4(3)(\\text-0.8)} \\over 2(3)} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-041",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle x = {2 \\pm \\sqrt{4+(12)(0.8)} \\over 6} \\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-ACTIVITY-042",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Solve These Ones",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Evaluating these solutions with a calculator gives decimal approximations -0.281 and 0.948.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L16-PRACTICE-001",
      "SourceSection": "Problem 1",
